00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:02.050
So let's get started here.

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:06.070
And this is how I think most
Kubernetes deployments look

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:07.030
like.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:10.090
If you're running
in the cloud, you're

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:13.420
typically using the logging
and monitoring tools that

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.100
come with that cloud platform.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.440
So for us, that's Stackdriver.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.090
If you're running
on premises, you're

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:24.130
probably running Elasticsearch
with Kibana, Prometheus,

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.050
and Grafana.

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:29.500
So two very different tools
for two very different

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.990
environments.

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:34.060
What we want to
envision is a world

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:37.030
where you have a
hybrid solution that

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:40.060
is consistent
across on premises,

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.070
as well as in the cloud.

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:47.590
So the way we think about hybrid
is, it's the same tooling--

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:50.020
it looks the same,
it behaves the same,

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.510
it's in the same
screen, particularly

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:56.470
if I have workloads running
in both different locations.

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:59.520
And so Kubernetes actually
gives us the ability to do this.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.280
The whole point
of Kubernetes was

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.170
so that we can abstract
away the infrastructure,

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.590
so we can have a consistent
environment no matter where

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:07.880
it's running.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.040
So we have this amazing
opportunity with Kubernetes

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:11.417
to do this.

00:03:11.417 --> 00:03:14.000
And so hopefully, we'll present
a couple of different patterns

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:14.670
to do that.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.780
But the way we
think about it is we

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.710
look at Stackdriver as the core
piece that can stitch together

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:25.820
to give you a on-premises view,
as well as a cloud-based view

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:26.810
of your workloads.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.380
But before we get too
much into the specifics,

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:36.710
I want to take a step back and
talk a little bit about how

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:40.400
Kubernetes internally works.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.910
So we have all these components
that rely on system metrics,

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:49.390
such as the horizontal pod
autoscaler, the scheduler.

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:52.840
We need to understand
your resource utilization

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.560
on your nodes for the
scheduler to work.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:59.230
We need to know system
metrics for the kubectl top,

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:02.630
so you can see all the resource
usage across the cluster.

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:06.110
But Kubernetes doesn't give
us a sophisticated logging

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:09.380
and monitoring system
out of the box,

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:13.270
but it does have these
system level components.

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:16.529
So this is implemented with
the metrics API and the metrics

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:18.100
server.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:21.130
And so these two
components work--

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.130
the metric server is
actually going and scraping

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:27.970
the system level metrics
from each individual node

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:29.690
and then publishing that up.

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:31.578
So let's take a look at
the nodes themselves.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:37.747
So on the nodes, obviously
we have the kubectl,

00:04:37.747 --> 00:04:38.830
and then we have cAdvisor.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:40.622
How many people are
familiar with cAdvisor?

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.300
A decent-- maybe a
handful, maybe 10 or so.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:46.490
That's good.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:51.420
So cAdvisor is a resource
usage collector for containers.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:54.300
It goes in, it auto discovers
all your containers,

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:59.250
and collects CPU, memory use,
network storage, et cetera.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:03.310
So the cAdvisor and the
kubectl, they work together.

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:06.120
These two components gather
all the metrics from the nodes,

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:08.160
and publish those
through the summary API.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:16.220
But going back to our bigger
picture, that cAdvisor

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:19.950
and the kubectl, this is what we
call the core metrics pipeline.

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:23.940
This is a core concept
inside of Kubernetes itself.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.370
But this is just for Kubernetes.

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:28.970
This has nothing to do with
your application workloads.

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:31.200
This is all just the
internal workings.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.320
So for my applications,
I actually

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:38.150
need a third party
monitoring solution.

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:39.530
So this is where we get to--

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:42.560
now we need to talk about,
OK, well, for GKE On-Prem,

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.140
what do we use for that third
party monitoring solution?

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:51.560
So let's take a look at that.

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:53.920
What we want to do
with GKE On-Prem

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.375
is get you the
flexibility that you want.

00:05:57.375 --> 00:05:58.750
We want you to
use the right tool

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:01.270
for whatever your purposes are.

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:04.340
So let's take a couple
different scenarios.

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:06.190
So maybe you have
GKE On-Prem, and it's

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:08.032
running your front end.

00:06:08.032 --> 00:06:10.860
And the back end is
somewhere off the cluster,

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.760
and you want to have end
to end visibility for that.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:18.710
So maybe you want to use an
existing Splunk environment,

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:21.950
or maybe you want the
ease of a fully integrated

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:24.720
cloud-based monitoring system.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.540
Stackdriver may be a
great option for you.

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:32.200
Maybe you're interested
in running workloads

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:35.470
in GCP, as well as on premises.

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:39.688
Then you need that view across
both different environments.

00:06:39.688 --> 00:06:41.230
So we want to get
you the flexibility

00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:43.843
to use whatever
tool you're used to.

00:06:43.843 --> 00:06:44.760
So a couple of things.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:47.790
So Kubernetes out of the box--

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:50.290
GKE On-Prem out of
the box, rather, it

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:52.440
comes pre-configured
with Stackdriver logging

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.670
and monitoring.

00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:57.310
We're also shipping a
Prometheus and Grafana

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:00.087
instance that's tailored
specifically for GKE On-Prem.

00:07:00.087 --> 00:07:02.420
And I'll talk to you a little
bit about what we've done.

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:08.080
Then we're working with our
partners, Splunk and Elastic,

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:10.600
to make sure that all these
systems work well together.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:14.720
All right.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:16.520
So let's dive into
Stackdriver just a little

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.645
bit, and then I'll cover
the Prometheus and Grafana

00:07:18.645 --> 00:07:20.740
at the end.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:23.665
So we're using three
different APIs here--

00:07:23.665 --> 00:07:25.040
three different
Stackdriver APIs.

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:28.000
The first one is monitoring.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.660
So running on the cluster, we
have a Prometheus collector.

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:34.980
We have a pod that's called
the Stackdriver Prometheus

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:36.010
Kubernetes Pod.

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:38.770
And inside of that, we
have Prometheus running.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:42.550
That's configured specifically
for collecting the Stackdriver

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:44.140
metrics.

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:49.310
Next to it is a pod, we call
it the Stackdriver collector.

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:52.480
So it's extracting
the Prometheus metrics

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:55.390
and shipping them over to the
Stackdriver monitoring API.

00:07:58.540 --> 00:08:01.420
We've done a couple of things
here on the Prometheus instance

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:03.730
to make sure it works
well in GKE On-Prem.

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:05.770
So we're doing things
like, we've expanded

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:07.840
the duration of the buffer.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:11.410
Because we want to make sure
that, in a network outage,

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:14.200
that the metrics are whole
inside of Stackdriver.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:16.720
So we've expanded
the buffer there.

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:18.860
A couple of other
things like that.

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:21.100
Now, this Prometheus
instance, this isn't something

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:22.960
you need to configure
or worry about.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.370
This is really just an
implementation detail.

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:31.830
So next, we have the logging.

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:33.413
The logging is pretty
straightforward.

00:08:33.413 --> 00:08:36.390
So we have a logging agent
running on each of the nodes.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:40.730
Those are aggregated up
into a logging collector.

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:44.150
It just so happens that we're
using Fluent Bit and Fluentd.

00:08:44.150 --> 00:08:45.650
Fluent Bit for
the logging agent,

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:49.117
and then Fluentd
for the collector.

00:08:49.117 --> 00:08:50.950
Again, that's just an
implementation detail.

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:58.050
The last piece is
the metadata agent.

00:08:58.050 --> 00:08:59.850
So metadata is pretty
important to us

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:02.430
because we want to give you a
better experience, rather than

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:04.480
just straight logs and metrics.

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:06.630
So with metadata, we
can stitch together

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:09.750
into a much better visibility
across the cluster,

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:12.210
because we actually have
some knowledge about what

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:16.000
is actually running
on your cluster.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:18.490
So with these three things--
so we have logs, metrics,

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:19.610
metadata--

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:22.362
these three things will give
you a much better experience.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:27.675
The last thing--

00:09:27.675 --> 00:09:29.300
I want to cover
Prometheus and Grafana.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:33.825
So not everybody wants to
use, necessarily, Stackdriver.

00:09:33.825 --> 00:09:36.200
Pretty much everybody that
raised their hand when I said,

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:37.970
who's using Prometheus
and Grafana.

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:41.247
So we're shipping Prometheus
and Grafana with GKE On-Prem.

00:09:41.247 --> 00:09:43.580
We've done a couple of things
to make sure it works well

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:45.302
inside the environment.

00:09:45.302 --> 00:09:46.760
The first thing we
wanted to do was

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:49.580
we wanted to give you
a multi-cluster view.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:51.910
Because with GKE
On-Prem, you're going

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:53.383
to have multiple clusters.

00:09:53.383 --> 00:09:55.550
Out of the box, you have
an admin cluster and a user

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:57.830
cluster.

00:09:57.830 --> 00:10:01.460
So what we've done is
configured Prometheus

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:04.100
to have a multi-cluster view.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:08.940
And you can choose the scope
that you want to look at.

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:11.145
So if you want to see the
multiple cluster view,

00:10:11.145 --> 00:10:12.770
you can use the
Grafana instance that's

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:15.620
running on the admin cluster.

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:17.790
If you want to see just
the user cluster-- so say,

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:21.282
for example, you have an
application specific cluster

00:10:21.282 --> 00:10:23.490
and you want to give that
out to an application team,

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:25.025
they can use that
Grafana instance.

00:10:29.618 --> 00:10:33.370
Then lastly, for those that
really enjoy using Grafana,

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:34.990
you can actually
build dashboards

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:38.710
that look at both the
Prometheus data for GKE On-Prem,

00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:42.600
as well as data from
your GKE clusters.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:49.000
Stackdriver is a native
data source for Grafana,

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:51.580
and so you can have
a dashboard looking

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:53.770
at both different clusters.

00:10:56.920 --> 00:10:57.420
All right.

00:10:57.420 --> 00:10:59.500
So that wraps it up.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:01.690
That gives you just
a general overview

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:03.820
of where we're at
with GKE On-Prem.

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:06.620
So a couple of different
options just to review--

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:09.360
cloud-based out of the
box with Stackdriver,

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:12.790
and then certainly Prometheus
and Grafana, as well as

00:11:12.790 --> 00:11:15.660
our partners.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:18.720
So with that, I'm
going to welcome up

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:22.320
Ruxanda and Shoucong, and
we'll walk through this demo.

00:11:26.176 --> 00:11:29.550
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:31.242 --> 00:11:32.950
RUXANDA DANETIU: Thank
you so much, Zach.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:37.870
So next, we're actually going
to go through the project.

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:39.640
So we're going to
actually look at how

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:42.340
this works and integrated
with Stackdriver.

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:46.780
So for the purpose of this, we
actually built a demo project.

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:48.730
This is the Petclinic.

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:52.540
Adorable pictures
always work at demos.

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:56.780
The concept behind these is that
you have a series of owners.

00:11:56.780 --> 00:11:59.710
So let's see who we
have in the owner list.

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:04.090
And we're going to try to figure
out if Shoucong is on the list

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:07.120
here, and see if we
can add a pet for him

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:12.082
so you can have an idea of
how these services look like.

00:12:12.082 --> 00:12:13.540
And while Shoucong
does that, let's

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:16.760
talk a little bit about what's
happening on the back end.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:18.580
So we're going to have
two clusters here.

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:22.540
One cluster is going to run
on GKE and on the cloud.

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:25.325
And one cluster is
going to be GKE On-Prem.

00:12:25.325 --> 00:12:26.700
And in this
scenario, we're going

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:29.860
to have the API gateway
with three microservices

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:33.640
in each location, and we're
going to push all of the data

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:36.460
to Cloud Spanner it on
GKE so we can actually

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:40.870
monitor both the GKE cluster
that's running on cloud

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:43.180
and the GKE cluster
On-Prem within

00:12:43.180 --> 00:12:45.640
the exact same visualization.

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:47.800
So once Shoucong
has completed this,

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:50.770
we're going to go and
see how we can actually

00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:52.960
get access to this information.

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:54.820
And throughout the
demo today, I'm

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:59.110
going to show you three main
pillars of the experience you

00:12:59.110 --> 00:13:00.670
get in Stackdriver.

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:05.680
The first one is going to talk
about Kubernetes monitoring out

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:09.850
of the box, and this is a beta
product that we have launched.

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:11.980
The second one, what
we're going to talk about

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:17.560
is going to be around
the custom experience

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:19.830
that you can actually build.

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:22.370
So you can do your own
custom dashboards as well.

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:24.370
And then we're going
to talk about alerting.

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:26.530
At the end, Shoucong
is going to take over,

00:13:26.530 --> 00:13:30.010
and he's going to give us the
idea of how you're actually

00:13:30.010 --> 00:13:31.220
doing a troubleshooting.

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:33.680
So right now, we're
going to add some load--

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:36.220
we're going to generate
some load to this app

00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:39.430
in order to be able to
see some alerts firing

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:40.810
and how we do the
troubleshooting

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:42.340
towards the end of the demo.

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:47.980
So he's doing this right now
in the GKE console for the GKE

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:49.960
cluster that's running on GCP.

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:52.120
You can see the two
clusters right there.

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:56.320
And then he's going to do the
exact same process for the GKE

00:13:56.320 --> 00:13:57.580
On-Prem cluster as well.

00:14:03.950 --> 00:14:04.550
Wonderful.

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:07.500
So let's go to the
Stackdriver UI.

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:10.760
As we're going to the
navigation within Stackdriver,

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:13.460
we can select
Kubernetes Monitoring.

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:15.750
This is the experience
that I mentioned before.

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:19.160
It is an out of the box
experience for all users

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:21.050
of Kubernetes and GKE.

00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:23.150
Right now, as we
are selecting this,

00:14:23.150 --> 00:14:25.910
this is a beta product
for Stackdriver.

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:27.740
Everything that you
see on this page,

00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:31.070
as long as you're using
GKE exactly as Zach

00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:34.820
mentioned, all of these
do not require a setup.

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:38.120
Everything gets collected
and gets reported for you.

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:41.510
So we have those two
clusters, one is on cloud

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:42.980
and one is on prem.

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:45.530
And I want to give you a
tour about the capabilities

00:14:45.530 --> 00:14:47.490
that you're getting
on this page.

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:51.260
So at the top of the page,
we have the alert timeline.

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:54.140
What we think is extremely
core to the experience is

00:14:54.140 --> 00:14:55.940
to know when things
get alerting,

00:14:55.940 --> 00:14:59.270
the troubleshooting
workflow is fundamental.

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:02.540
And here, we want to actually
showcase all of the alerts

00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:05.990
that you have within your
Kubernetes environment that

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:08.910
span both cloud and on prem.

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:11.303
So if you hover
any of these cards,

00:15:11.303 --> 00:15:12.470
it's almost like a heat map.

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:15.740
The more alerts you have,
the darker the shade of red.

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:19.340
And you get information about
the alerts that have fired.

00:15:19.340 --> 00:15:22.850
Underneath this, we have these
three tabs, Infrastructure,

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:24.990
Workloads, and Services.

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:27.710
And we spent a lot of
time talking to customers

00:15:27.710 --> 00:15:30.380
and getting to understand
what are the different flows,

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:32.690
personas, and use cases.

00:15:32.690 --> 00:15:35.060
And we realized
that in some cases,

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:38.120
operators and developers
may have different interests

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:39.470
or entry points.

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:41.330
So we built these
three different tabs

00:15:41.330 --> 00:15:45.110
to satisfy somebody that's
really interested in focusing

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:48.350
on nodes and clusters and
an infrastructure approach,

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:50.360
but we also want to
cater to developers

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:53.150
that are more interested
in deployments and services

00:15:53.150 --> 00:15:54.210
as well.

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:56.210
So this gives a
quick entry point

00:15:56.210 --> 00:15:58.790
to all of those experiences.

00:15:58.790 --> 00:16:01.070
We are starting with
the infrastructure tab.

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:04.460
And the two clusters
are, as you can see here,

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:09.230
we are going to open a GKE
cluster running on cloud.

00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:14.670
The table itself has almost
a hierarchy built into it.

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:17.120
And while we know that
Kubernetes and GKE does not

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:21.140
have a hierarchy, per
se, but the metadata

00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:24.080
allows us to read
all of these labels

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:26.360
and actually build
an intuitive way

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:28.340
of reporting this information.

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:30.800
So you can see your
cluster, the nodes in it,

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:34.520
the pods within that node,
all the way to the container.

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:37.280
And this level of drill
down can be really helpful

00:16:37.280 --> 00:16:39.320
as you're trying to
do troubleshooting

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:41.450
or you're trying to
find particular things,

00:16:41.450 --> 00:16:44.480
so you have the groupings
to help you to navigate this

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:46.400
really easily.

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:47.990
At the same time,
the table itself

00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:50.180
gives you at a
glance information

00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:53.720
around the resource, the
number of pods that are ready,

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:57.980
the number of incidents, the
CPU utilization and memory.

00:16:57.980 --> 00:17:00.140
On the workloads page,
we have a hierarchy

00:17:00.140 --> 00:17:04.730
that spans clusters,
namespaces, deployments, pods,

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:05.869
and containers.

00:17:05.869 --> 00:17:09.290
So you see that there
are different ways where

00:17:09.290 --> 00:17:11.900
things can get clusters,
and the hierarchy is really

00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:14.569
important as a way of
getting to the drill

00:17:14.569 --> 00:17:17.150
down that's more
significant to the workflow

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:19.010
that you're interested in.

00:17:19.010 --> 00:17:24.003
And we have-- let's pick for
the API gateway for deployment.

00:17:24.003 --> 00:17:25.670
Let's click on one
of those and actually

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:28.170
look at some of the
details of a pod.

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:31.610
So for every single
entry within this table,

00:17:31.610 --> 00:17:34.070
you get the details panel
that gives you information,

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:37.390
gives out of the box metrics
that are already created.

00:17:37.390 --> 00:17:40.420
You get these charts
pre-populated for you.

00:17:40.420 --> 00:17:42.320
So it requires no setup.

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:44.970
We have an integration
with logging as well.

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:46.760
So you're going to
get a high level

00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:51.020
histogram of the logs
for this particular pod,

00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:54.120
and you're going to
get a selection of logs

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:57.150
filtered by severity
for this pod as well.

00:17:57.150 --> 00:17:59.310
And of course,
that's never enough.

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:02.180
So you're going to be
able to very quickly click

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:05.220
on the entire
logging experience.

00:18:05.220 --> 00:18:07.530
The context is going
to stay the same.

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:10.790
So here, we are in the full
logging experience in the GCP

00:18:10.790 --> 00:18:15.240
console, but we are only looking
at that particular cluster,

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:18.080
at that particular pod
that we are evaluating,

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:19.980
and that particular time span.

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:23.720
So you can go and end up doing
your troubleshooting there

00:18:23.720 --> 00:18:25.610
and do more filtering.

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:28.680
And then we have details
information as well.

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:32.180
So as we are going through
all of this information,

00:18:32.180 --> 00:18:33.920
the details are
really important,

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:36.560
and you're able
to go and get logs

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:40.790
and metrics for every single
entry within those tables.

00:18:40.790 --> 00:18:44.160
And of course, we have a deep
integration with the Kubernetes

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:45.650
and GKE teams.

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:47.720
So you can go in
and actually get

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:52.320
a lot more detailed information
from the GKE page in context.

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:54.560
So when we go back
to Stackdriver,

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:58.640
we can actually see the
third column is Services.

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:01.040
This gives you an
ability to actually

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:06.880
go through the entire hierarchy
with services as well,

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:09.900
and gives you information
within the same span

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:17.490
of cluster, namespace,
service pod, and containers.

00:19:17.490 --> 00:19:21.810
So let's go back to
infrastructure for a second.

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:25.600
The table itself has
an embedded hierarchy,

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:27.330
and it's interesting
to navigate.

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:30.540
But one of the things that is
sometimes problematic, when

00:19:30.540 --> 00:19:33.000
you have hundreds
of these entities,

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:36.000
it's really hard to
understand patterns

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:39.240
and being able to come up
with insights of the way

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:42.300
that, maybe, there is a trend
out there that you're not

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:44.280
able to see in a table format.

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:47.880
So we build this
bubble visualization,

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:50.850
mostly because we really
like bubble visualizations.

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:51.840
No.

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:56.040
But the idea is that it's going
to empower you to use metrics.

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:59.280
So we have a CPU
usage on the x-axis,

00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:01.860
and on the y-axis, memory usage.

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:04.980
Each bubble represents a node.

00:20:04.980 --> 00:20:07.410
The size of the
bubble represents

00:20:07.410 --> 00:20:12.090
the number of pods in that
node-- so how big the node is.

00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:16.680
And then the color
represents the health.

00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:20.040
So if it's a light gray,
it means that it's healthy,

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:23.020
and red means that
it's an incident.

00:20:23.020 --> 00:20:25.830
And as you can see, there are
all of these different clusters

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:28.350
that are forming, there
are all of these patterns

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:29.640
of information.

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:32.550
This is an exploratory
tool that we're

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:34.260
launching in beta as well.

00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:38.370
And we are trying to make
this as a way of using

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:40.770
the data that you have, the
information that you have,

00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:44.940
and come up with interesting
insights about trends that

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:46.800
are happening across these.

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:48.780
It's important to mention
that right now, we're

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:54.010
looking at nodes that span
both cloud and on prem.

00:20:54.010 --> 00:20:56.070
So it's a fascinating
way of understanding

00:20:56.070 --> 00:20:58.670
this behavior, where,
at the end of the day,

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:01.920
we want to get to a position
in, especially with Anthos,

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:06.210
is that you shouldn't care if
something is on prem or on GCP,

00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:08.610
and you need to have a
single tool that manages all

00:21:08.610 --> 00:21:11.430
of these things for you so
your monitoring solution is

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:13.350
in one space.

00:21:13.350 --> 00:21:16.530
So far, we talked about
the out of the box

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:21.210
experience for Kubernetes
and GKE within Stackdriver.

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:24.000
And while we understand that
every single out of the box

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:27.937
experience out there is
amazing up to 80% of the time,

00:21:27.937 --> 00:21:29.520
and then you're going
to have some use

00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:32.340
cases where you're going to
need to build something custom.

00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:35.790
So Stackdriver allows
you to get access

00:21:35.790 --> 00:21:38.550
to all of this information
and build your own custom

00:21:38.550 --> 00:21:40.300
experience as well.

00:21:40.300 --> 00:21:42.600
So what we developed
is this tool called

00:21:42.600 --> 00:21:45.390
Metric Explorer that
gives you the ability

00:21:45.390 --> 00:21:50.220
to actually query all of the
metrics that are coming in--

00:21:50.220 --> 00:21:52.920
all of your system
level metrics.

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:56.610
And these metrics come in
either from GCP on from on prem.

00:21:56.610 --> 00:21:58.455
You find them within
the exact same space.

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:03.990
As you can see here, there
is a lot of information.

00:22:03.990 --> 00:22:07.110
We have a lot of
lines on that chart,

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:08.850
and it might not be readable.

00:22:08.850 --> 00:22:11.040
So maybe we want
to apply filtering.

00:22:11.040 --> 00:22:14.070
So you can benefit from
using advanced filtering.

00:22:14.070 --> 00:22:17.430
At the same time, we
can group things by,

00:22:17.430 --> 00:22:19.650
we can add a lot
of functionality

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:23.590
and aggregate this
information as well.

00:22:23.590 --> 00:22:26.980
And one of the things that
we build is that sometimes--

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:28.770
we try to do a
good filtering, we

00:22:28.770 --> 00:22:31.120
do a good job of bringing
in a lot of data.

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:33.810
But it's too much data
to make it meaningful.

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:37.320
So we've introduced
modes and options for you

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:40.080
to do an outlier
mode, so you can

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:44.670
pick to always show the top
five outliers within your space,

00:22:44.670 --> 00:22:48.090
or top 10, or top 15,
the number that you care,

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:50.460
and make these charts
really meaningful for you

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:53.190
when you go in to know
where the problems are,

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:55.990
so you can investigate
a lot faster.

00:22:55.990 --> 00:22:58.750
And as you can see here, there
is a lot of trial and error.

00:22:58.750 --> 00:23:01.410
So we can spend
quite a few minutes

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:04.440
until we figure out what
are the right metrics, what

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:07.225
are the right filters, until
you build your great chart.

00:23:07.225 --> 00:23:09.350
And you don't want to do
that every single time you

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:10.890
log into Stackdriver.

00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:12.690
So we have the
ability for you to be

00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:16.560
able to save these charts, and
save them in a new dashboard,

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:19.140
add them to an
existing dashboard.

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:22.005
These charts can be easily
shared through your URL

00:23:22.005 --> 00:23:26.740
with all of your colleagues,
so you share the same context.

00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:30.240
So let's look at
a custom dashboard

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:35.010
next so we can actually
see this capability live.

00:23:35.010 --> 00:23:39.030
This is a hybrid dashboard,
so every single chart

00:23:39.030 --> 00:23:41.430
that you see on
this graph displays

00:23:41.430 --> 00:23:47.070
data that is collected from
both Cloud and On-Prem GKE.

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:51.520
So a lot of this information
is being brought to you

00:23:51.520 --> 00:23:53.590
in one single dashboard.

00:23:53.590 --> 00:23:55.800
You don't have to create
multiple dashboards,

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:57.900
you don't have to go
to multiple consoles

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:00.130
to have different experiences.

00:24:00.130 --> 00:24:01.860
Everything can
come in one place.

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:05.630
You can easily add more charts
and make this customizable.

00:24:05.630 --> 00:24:08.040
And then you can easily
play and delete things,

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:10.970
so you can make this space
really meaningful to your team.

00:24:13.580 --> 00:24:17.000
We talked about the
custom experience.

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:22.040
Next, I want us to focus a
little bit around alerting.

00:24:22.040 --> 00:24:25.940
We know at the end of the
day, monitoring proactively--

00:24:25.940 --> 00:24:27.740
it's time consuming,
it's something

00:24:27.740 --> 00:24:30.770
that you won't be able to
just spend all of your day

00:24:30.770 --> 00:24:32.450
looking in front of
a screen and waiting

00:24:32.450 --> 00:24:33.960
for something to go wrong.

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:36.710
So we need to be really
smart about our alerts.

00:24:36.710 --> 00:24:40.440
So we created an integrated
alerting experience.

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:43.700
So let's try to start the
process of creating an alert.

00:24:43.700 --> 00:24:46.610
As you can see, the interface
that you have access to right

00:24:46.610 --> 00:24:51.680
now is the same interface
that we showed earlier, is

00:24:51.680 --> 00:24:53.810
the Metric Explorer Interface.

00:24:53.810 --> 00:24:57.410
You have you have the ability--
once you get to know the system

00:24:57.410 --> 00:24:59.900
and start playing with
Metric Explorer, that

00:24:59.900 --> 00:25:02.480
is the one-stop shop for
creating your dashboards,

00:25:02.480 --> 00:25:03.890
creating your alerts.

00:25:03.890 --> 00:25:05.300
You're using the same workflow.

00:25:05.300 --> 00:25:07.130
We want to make
sure that you don't

00:25:07.130 --> 00:25:10.760
have to have a ramp
up of learning curve

00:25:10.760 --> 00:25:12.840
for each and every experience.

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:16.700
So you have the exact same
metric capability, filters,

00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:21.260
all of these things are brought
in within the same workflow.

00:25:21.260 --> 00:25:24.110
So of course, you
can edit these,

00:25:24.110 --> 00:25:28.805
you can add notifications, you
can add critical documentation.

00:25:28.805 --> 00:25:29.930
These are really important.

00:25:29.930 --> 00:25:32.780
We really encourage
all of our customers

00:25:32.780 --> 00:25:35.960
to use documentation
heavily in their alerts,

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:40.740
and link to dashboards, and
provide links to books as well.

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:43.810
So let's look at some of the
incidents that we just created.

00:25:43.810 --> 00:25:46.490
So these will be displayed,
so you can actually

00:25:46.490 --> 00:25:48.920
have a list of all
of your incidents.

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:53.040
And these can span
alerting policies,

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:55.490
and these can span both
the environments that you

00:25:55.490 --> 00:25:58.400
have on cloud and on prem.

00:25:58.400 --> 00:26:00.710
And now that we've covered--

00:26:00.710 --> 00:26:02.630
just to bring
everything together.

00:26:02.630 --> 00:26:07.190
We talked at the beginning about
the out of the box experience.

00:26:07.190 --> 00:26:10.250
We're hoping that that
experience gives you an option

00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:14.960
to have visibility without any
setup and be able to sustain,

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:18.650
probably, the large
majority of the use cases.

00:26:18.650 --> 00:26:20.720
Then we talked about
the custom option.

00:26:20.720 --> 00:26:22.970
If you want to build your
custom dashboards that

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:25.040
are hybrid, that
span both clouds,

00:26:25.040 --> 00:26:26.720
you have that option as well.

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:30.200
And how to build a really,
really smart alerting

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:35.240
that can help you navigate
how not to get woken up

00:26:35.240 --> 00:26:37.770
at 3 o'clock in the morning.

00:26:37.770 --> 00:26:39.770
But because we
created these alerts

00:26:39.770 --> 00:26:41.870
and we added some
load to the app

00:26:41.870 --> 00:26:45.560
at the beginning of the demo, I
think Shoucong just got paged.

00:26:45.560 --> 00:26:48.582
And he's going to show us what
happens once you get an alert,

00:26:48.582 --> 00:26:49.790
and how we troubleshoot this.

00:26:49.790 --> 00:26:51.380
So Shoucong.

00:26:51.380 --> 00:26:52.400
SHOUCONG CHEN: OK.

00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:53.540
Thanks, Ruxanda.

00:26:53.540 --> 00:26:54.620
So this is Shoucong.

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:57.920
I'm going to give you
a demo about where

00:26:57.920 --> 00:26:59.780
a typical, classical
troubleshooting

00:26:59.780 --> 00:27:02.630
scenario in our daily work.

00:27:02.630 --> 00:27:05.330
So as a SWE--

00:27:05.330 --> 00:27:08.690
so we're usually
[INAUDIBLE] is that when

00:27:08.690 --> 00:27:13.455
your application becomes more
and more used by customer,

00:27:13.455 --> 00:27:15.080
you're receiving more
and more traffic.

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:17.330
And in the meantime,
some of your server

00:27:17.330 --> 00:27:22.370
become slow and unresponsive,
and it's usually the moment

00:27:22.370 --> 00:27:24.330
that some on-call
engineer were jumping

00:27:24.330 --> 00:27:26.240
and they figure out
what's happening.

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:30.230
And we want to understand,
is it due to a bad machine,

00:27:30.230 --> 00:27:34.320
due to a bad deployment, or
due to something misconfigured?

00:27:34.320 --> 00:27:37.310
And it's become more
and more complicated,

00:27:37.310 --> 00:27:41.360
especially when you
have distributed set up.

00:27:41.360 --> 00:27:44.870
Also like a hybrid setup,
you have GKE On-Cloud,

00:27:44.870 --> 00:27:48.680
you have Kubernetes
On-Prem, or GKE On-Prem,

00:27:48.680 --> 00:27:52.340
and you're also distributed
across many different areas.

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:56.750
That is the scenario
we're trying to demo here.

00:27:56.750 --> 00:28:00.500
So as you may recall from
maybe 10 minutes ago,

00:28:00.500 --> 00:28:03.110
when Ruxanda
started her demo, we

00:28:03.110 --> 00:28:07.700
generated some traffic to
both of my GKE cluster and GKE

00:28:07.700 --> 00:28:10.010
On-Prem cluster.

00:28:10.010 --> 00:28:14.030
So whilst she is demoing,
as the on-call engineer

00:28:14.030 --> 00:28:17.460
of this Petclinic website,
I have been paged.

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:21.530
So this is the
alert I've received.

00:28:21.530 --> 00:28:24.470
Stackdriver sends me an alert,
talking about something--

00:28:24.470 --> 00:28:28.670
HTTP sever request count
for 500 is too high.

00:28:28.670 --> 00:28:32.710
And based on the documentation,
I know this means too many--

00:28:32.710 --> 00:28:35.570
500 has been returned
for my services.

00:28:35.570 --> 00:28:39.320
This is a little bit concerning
to me because returning 500

00:28:39.320 --> 00:28:43.860
means my customer may be
experiencing this outage now.

00:28:43.860 --> 00:28:46.980
So let's take a look at
what incident detail page

00:28:46.980 --> 00:28:48.530
Stackdriver tells me.

00:28:48.530 --> 00:28:50.810
So as you can see
here, we are receiving

00:28:50.810 --> 00:28:55.110
around 12 requests
per second of 500

00:28:55.110 --> 00:28:58.940
for the service API gateway.

00:28:58.940 --> 00:29:00.860
This is a little
bit concerning to me

00:29:00.860 --> 00:29:05.860
because API gateway is the
interface of my application.

00:29:05.860 --> 00:29:12.060
So as a SWE, I go to the
predefined custom dashboard,

00:29:12.060 --> 00:29:16.680
which has almost all of
the important metrics

00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:18.930
I'm curious about
my hybrid setup.

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:22.920
So as you can see on
the top two charts--

00:29:22.920 --> 00:29:27.060
the first chart on the top left
corner, this is the hybrid--

00:29:27.060 --> 00:29:32.250
the request counts go to both
clusters in my hybrid setup.

00:29:32.250 --> 00:29:36.480
As you can see, I receive
around 150 requests

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:40.920
go to my GKE On-Prem cluster,
and then around 200 requests

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:42.670
go to my GKE cluster.

00:29:42.670 --> 00:29:45.450
This is working as intended
because this is how

00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:50.220
my load balancer configures.

00:29:50.220 --> 00:29:53.410
So let's take a look
at the top right chart.

00:29:53.410 --> 00:29:55.140
So this is the error rates.

00:29:55.140 --> 00:29:57.190
So from this chart,
it's giving me

00:29:57.190 --> 00:30:01.590
a very strong signal that
something is wrong in my GKE

00:30:01.590 --> 00:30:02.790
On-Prem cluster.

00:30:02.790 --> 00:30:05.760
Because we can see
here, all of the 500

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:08.730
are coming from my
GKE On-Prem cluster.

00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:12.960
So as a developer,
my first interaction

00:30:12.960 --> 00:30:16.140
is I tried to dive into
this GKE On-Prem cluster

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:18.600
to debug what the issue will be.

00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:23.040
But as the Google [INAUDIBLE]
best practice tell me,

00:30:23.040 --> 00:30:25.020
the first thing I need
to do at this moment

00:30:25.020 --> 00:30:27.040
is to mitigate the issue.

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:30.030
So one of the various
classical ways,

00:30:30.030 --> 00:30:33.150
we just [INAUDIBLE]
this GKE On-Prem cluster

00:30:33.150 --> 00:30:36.360
and failover the
traffic to GKE cluster.

00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:38.520
Before we do that, I
have one more thing

00:30:38.520 --> 00:30:42.250
I want to confirm for
additional safety.

00:30:42.250 --> 00:30:46.840
So let's go to Kubernetes
monitoring view.

00:30:46.840 --> 00:30:49.410
The thing I want to
confirm is, if I fail,

00:30:49.410 --> 00:30:51.240
that means my GKE
cluster will receive

00:30:51.240 --> 00:30:53.550
the double size of the traffic.

00:30:53.550 --> 00:30:57.570
I want to make sure my GKE
cluster can sustain the load.

00:30:57.570 --> 00:31:01.350
So that's why I come to
these infrastructure view.

00:31:01.350 --> 00:31:06.830
As I can see here, CPU and
memory usage for both cluster

00:31:06.830 --> 00:31:09.930
are around 20%.

00:31:09.930 --> 00:31:14.670
If I expand the GKE cluster,
I see there have six nodes,

00:31:14.670 --> 00:31:18.450
and each of the nodes is
running around 20% CPU

00:31:18.450 --> 00:31:22.740
utilization and around 20%
to 30% of memory utilization.

00:31:22.740 --> 00:31:27.870
So you still have plenty of
space, so I can failover.

00:31:27.870 --> 00:31:31.350
I think that's a safe
move at this moment.

00:31:31.350 --> 00:31:34.980
So let's go to this
GKE On-Prem cluster.

00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:38.520
I just ran the traffic
into this cluster.

00:31:38.520 --> 00:31:42.490
And then I go to this
Kubernetes cloud SS edge,

00:31:42.490 --> 00:31:43.910
and then I just double the size.

00:31:43.910 --> 00:31:45.870
This is to simulate
the failover.

00:31:45.870 --> 00:31:48.720
So in real world, we will
have more of the ones load

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:50.670
balancer configure will do.

00:31:50.670 --> 00:31:53.250
But in this demo, we just
simulate it this way.

00:31:56.940 --> 00:32:01.270
So as we go back
into this dashboard,

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:04.470
usually the load balancer will
take around one or two minutes

00:32:04.470 --> 00:32:06.610
for the traffic to migrate.

00:32:06.610 --> 00:32:09.450
So eventually, we will
see the On-Prem cluster

00:32:09.450 --> 00:32:13.130
traffic goes down, and then
GKE cluster traffic goes up.

00:32:13.130 --> 00:32:15.480
But we will not wait the
time here to wait for that.

00:32:15.480 --> 00:32:17.540
We will go back after
I finish my demo

00:32:17.540 --> 00:32:20.760
to verify this is the behavior.

00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:26.250
So I believe the current
outage is reasonably mitigated.

00:32:26.250 --> 00:32:30.280
As a developer, I want to debug
the root cause of the issue.

00:32:30.280 --> 00:32:32.790
So that's the moment I
go to my Service tab,

00:32:32.790 --> 00:32:36.480
because that's the
thing that is alerting.

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:40.960
I see this incident color
has given me very good hints.

00:32:40.960 --> 00:32:43.800
Something is wrong
in On-Prem cluster.

00:32:43.800 --> 00:32:47.130
This is another way we
can narrow down the issue.

00:32:47.130 --> 00:32:50.340
And we go to this incident
color keep telling me

00:32:50.340 --> 00:32:53.440
where I should focus
my attention to.

00:32:53.440 --> 00:32:54.870
So this is the API gateway.

00:32:54.870 --> 00:32:58.020
I should take a
look at this part.

00:32:58.020 --> 00:33:00.810
So let's open this
detailed view.

00:33:00.810 --> 00:33:03.240
I see there's an open incident,
and there's too many--

00:33:03.240 --> 00:33:06.410
500 has been returned
from this container.

00:33:06.410 --> 00:33:09.720
Let's look at the metrics
for this specific path.

00:33:09.720 --> 00:33:11.490
As you can see,
container restart--

00:33:11.490 --> 00:33:15.420
there's nothing restart,
which seems great.

00:33:15.420 --> 00:33:19.200
Memory utilization is flat
because this app is not

00:33:19.200 --> 00:33:20.560
memory heavy.

00:33:20.560 --> 00:33:24.150
Also, data is starting
in the Cloud Spanner.

00:33:24.150 --> 00:33:28.740
For CPU utilization, we see
from around 15 or 10 minutes

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:31.860
ago that have some CPU spike.

00:33:31.860 --> 00:33:36.290
That's expected because we
generate a lot of traffic.

00:33:36.290 --> 00:33:38.510
Storage usage is
also flat because we

00:33:38.510 --> 00:33:40.160
don't write anything.

00:33:40.160 --> 00:33:42.000
Network usage is high.

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:45.500
This is also caused by
the traffic increase.

00:33:45.500 --> 00:33:48.005
So just looking
at the metrics, it

00:33:48.005 --> 00:33:50.130
seems everything
is looking great.

00:33:50.130 --> 00:33:53.930
It means my Kubernetes
infrastructure

00:33:53.930 --> 00:33:55.670
is running healthily.

00:33:55.670 --> 00:33:57.500
Then what could
be the issue here?

00:33:57.500 --> 00:34:00.530
At this moment,
this is the moment

00:34:00.530 --> 00:34:03.160
where a log can help us a lot.

00:34:03.160 --> 00:34:07.370
So if you go to the Log tab,
we see the log rates here.

00:34:07.370 --> 00:34:09.830
This gives me a very
obvious signal something

00:34:09.830 --> 00:34:14.719
is wrong, because my application
is writing a lot of error logs.

00:34:14.719 --> 00:34:19.730
As you can see, this is around
40%, or one third of logs

00:34:19.730 --> 00:34:21.650
are returning as error log.

00:34:21.650 --> 00:34:25.010
This is very suspicious
because normally, we

00:34:25.010 --> 00:34:27.020
don't write so many error logs.

00:34:27.020 --> 00:34:29.639
And we go to the snippet,
we see there's indeed

00:34:29.639 --> 00:34:31.190
some error logs here.

00:34:31.190 --> 00:34:33.590
Let's go to logs [INAUDIBLE]
to see more details.

00:34:36.699 --> 00:34:38.670
So this is already
an expanded arrow.

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:46.840
For the detail here, we can see
this is Java Runtime exception

00:34:46.840 --> 00:34:48.469
have been thrown.

00:34:48.469 --> 00:34:52.550
And it gives me the
full stack trace.

00:34:52.550 --> 00:34:56.530
So I can go to where the
thing that's happening

00:34:56.530 --> 00:35:00.190
in the very concrete detail.

00:35:00.190 --> 00:35:02.630
So let's see what this means.

00:35:02.630 --> 00:35:03.460
Let's just fetch.

00:35:06.960 --> 00:35:08.600
Fetch [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:08.600 --> 00:35:09.685
Let's go to the code.

00:35:12.420 --> 00:35:15.690
So this is where
my code base is.

00:35:15.690 --> 00:35:20.850
So I just do a grab of
a past error message.

00:35:20.850 --> 00:35:22.590
I can see this is
something that is

00:35:22.590 --> 00:35:26.040
written in the API gateway
controller in Java,

00:35:26.040 --> 00:35:28.110
and in line 54.

00:35:28.110 --> 00:35:29.630
Let's just open the file.

00:35:34.350 --> 00:35:38.370
Unable to fetch.

00:35:38.370 --> 00:35:42.720
So at this moment, it's
very quite clear to me

00:35:42.720 --> 00:35:45.150
this error message
or this 500 error

00:35:45.150 --> 00:35:49.140
has been thrown because
somebody write this line of very

00:35:49.140 --> 00:35:54.420
suspicious functionality.

00:35:54.420 --> 00:35:56.430
It's basically just like a--

00:35:56.430 --> 00:35:59.790
it was occurring before
returning a successful request.

00:35:59.790 --> 00:36:03.390
So there's a 50% chance
that you will not

00:36:03.390 --> 00:36:11.580
be able to get a correct
result. So here, this moment, we

00:36:11.580 --> 00:36:13.650
have already identified
the root cause.

00:36:13.650 --> 00:36:18.630
The root cause is somebody
introduced a bug into the code,

00:36:18.630 --> 00:36:22.380
and then deployed it into
my GKE On-Prem cluster.

00:36:22.380 --> 00:36:26.010
So the next steps for
me are very clear.

00:36:26.010 --> 00:36:29.400
I will just fix the
bug in the code,

00:36:29.400 --> 00:36:32.790
redeploy into the
GKE On-Prem cluster,

00:36:32.790 --> 00:36:36.470
and then [INAUDIBLE]
the GKE On-Prem cluster.

00:36:36.470 --> 00:36:40.820
Then we can make our
application back to normal.

00:36:40.820 --> 00:36:44.470
So this is the end of the demo.

00:36:44.470 --> 00:36:48.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

